course: history
lesson: 5
title: Ancient Egypt: the Nile and the pharaohs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Egypt is mostly desert, yet one of the greatest civilisations grew there, all thanks to one river, the Nile. Every summer it flooded and left rich black mud along its banks, perfect for crops.
explain: Imagine a long green ribbon of fields on a huge sandy map: that ribbon is where nearly all Egyptians lived. That is why Egypt was called the gift of the Nile.
explain: About 5,100 years ago the southern and northern lands were joined into one kingdom, ruled by a king called the pharaoh, whom Egyptians believed was partly a god.
explain: Pharaohs ruled for about 3,000 years: Hatshepsut, a woman who ruled as king, Tutankhamun, a boy king, Ramesses the Great, and at the very end Cleopatra. Meanwhile most people farmed, ate bread and onions, and played board games like senet.
fact: Egypt is mostly desert, but the Nile flooded every summer and left rich black mud along its banks, perfect for crops.
fact: Egyptians had three seasons: the flood, the growing time and the harvest.
fact: About 5,100 years ago a king remembered as Narmer or Menes joined Upper Egypt in the south and Lower Egypt in the north into one kingdom.
fact: The pharaoh was the king of Egypt, and Egyptians believed he was partly a god who kept order in the world.
fact: Hatshepsut was a woman who ruled as pharaoh about 3,500 years ago; she sometimes wore a king's false beard and sent a famous trading trip to the land of Punt.
fact: Tutankhamun became pharaoh as a boy of about nine and died at about nineteen; he was not very important in his own time but is famous because his tomb was found almost untouched in 1922.
fact: The curse of Tutankhamun is a newspaper legend; most people who opened the tomb lived long lives.
fact: Ramesses II ruled for 66 years, built huge temples such as Abu Simbel and had more than a hundred children.
fact: Cleopatra VII, the last pharaoh, lived about 2,050 years ago, spoke many languages and tried to keep Egypt free by allying with Roman leaders.
fact: Cleopatra lived closer in time to us than to the building of the Great Pyramid.
fact: Most Egyptians were farmers who ate bread, onions and fish and drank thick beer, and children played with balls, dolls and board games like senet.
fact: Egyptians worshipped many gods, often shown with animal heads, such as Anubis with a jackal's head and Horus with a falcon's head.
exercise: Why was Egypt called the gift of the Nile? | The river's floods made the desert land fertile | The Nile was given to Egypt as a present by Rome | The river was full of gold | The Nile was a present to the pharaoh from the gods
exercise: What did the Nile leave behind after its yearly flood? | Rich black mud | White salt crusts | Tall sand dunes | Ice and snow
exercise: How many seasons did the Egyptians have? | Three | Four | Two | Twelve
exercise: What were the Egyptian seasons? | Flood, growing time and harvest | Spring, summer, autumn and winter | Rain and snow | Hot, cold and windy
exercise: About how long ago were Upper and Lower Egypt joined? | About 5,100 years ago | About 510 years ago | About 51,000 years ago | About 51 years ago
exercise: What did Egyptians believe about the pharaoh? | He was partly a god who kept order | He was just an ordinary farmer like them | He was chosen by a vote | He came from another planet
exercise: What was special about Hatshepsut? | She was a woman who ruled as pharaoh | She built the first pyramid all by herself | She invented hieroglyphs | She was the last pharaoh of Egypt
exercise: Where did Hatshepsut send a famous trading trip? | To the land of Punt | To Rome | To faraway China | To Britain
exercise: How old was Tutankhamun when he became pharaoh? | About nine | About forty | About ninety | About nineteen
exercise: Why is Tutankhamun so famous today? | His tomb was found almost untouched in 1922 | He conquered the whole world before he was twenty | He built the Great Pyramid | He ruled for 66 years
exercise: How long did Ramesses II rule? | 66 years | Only 6 years | 166 years | Only 6 months
exercise: Which huge temple did Ramesses II build? | Abu Simbel | The Parthenon in Athens | Stonehenge | The Colosseum
exercise: Who was the last pharaoh of Egypt? | Cleopatra VII | Tutankhamun | Ramesses II | Hatshepsut
exercise: What did most ordinary Egyptians eat? | Bread, onions and fish | Pizza and chips | Rice and noodles | Potatoes and sweetcorn
exercise: What was senet? | A board game | A kind of boat | A god with a cat's head | A type of bread
exercise: Which god had a jackal's head? | Anubis | Horus | Zeus | Thor
test: my sister says Tut's curse killed everyone who opened the tomb. right? | No, it is a newspaper legend; most lived long lives | Yes, all of them died that same year, one after another | Yes, but only the dogs died | No, the tomb was never opened
test: What would happen to Egypt's farms if the Nile stopped flooding? | The fields would not get new mud and water | The crops would grow twice as fast as before | Egypt would become a jungle | Nothing, as it rained every day there anyway
test: Which is true about when Cleopatra lived? | She lived closer to us than to the building of the Great Pyramid | She lived at the same time as the pyramid builders | She lived before the first farms | She lived in the Middle Ages
test: A cartoon shows Egyptians eating burgers. What did they really eat most? | Bread, onions and fish | Burgers and chips | Sushi and rice | Tacos and beans
test: Why did Hatshepsut sometimes wear a false beard? | It was part of a king's royal look | She was hiding from robbers in the city | Beards kept the hot sun off her face | It was a joke at a party
test: Why did nearly all Egyptians live close to the Nile? | The rest of the land was mostly desert | The river was the only place with roads | The pharaoh banned living in the desert | They liked to swim every day
test: ugh tutankhamun was the greatest pharaoh ever wasn't he? | No, he was a minor king made famous by his tomb | Yes, he ruled for 66 years | Yes, he built all the pyramids and the Sphinx as well | No, he was never a pharaoh
test: Horus is shown with a bird's head. Which bird? | A falcon | A night owl | A chicken | A green parrot
test: Before Narmer, Egypt had two lands. What were they called? | Upper Egypt and Lower Egypt | North Egypt and East Egypt | Big Egypt and Little Egypt | Old Egypt and New Egypt
test: Cleopatra made friends with Roman leaders. What was she trying to do? | Keep Egypt free | Become a Roman senator | Move Egypt to Italy | Start the Olympic Games
test: More than a hundred children, a 66-year reign, Abu Simbel. Who? | Ramesses II | Tutankhamun | Queen Cleopatra | King Narmer
exam: Identify the principal reason for which ancient Egypt has been designated the gift of the Nile. | The annual inundation deposited fertile soil in an otherwise arid land | The river conveyed gold from the mountains | The river constituted a gift from Rome | The river prevented all invasions
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the so-called curse of Tutankhamun. | It is a journalistic legend without foundation | It is attested by the deaths of all the excavators | It is recorded upon the walls of the tomb | It was proclaimed by Ramesses II
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises Hatshepsut? | A woman who exercised the office of pharaoh | The final ruler of Egypt prior to Roman rule | The builder of the Great Pyramid | A deity represented with the head of a jackal
exam: Ascertain the duration of the reign of Ramesses II. | Sixty-six years | Six years | One hundred and sixty-six years | Nineteen years
